    en: "This unit introduces K1 children to food, drink, sharing, eating routines, and simple care through a non-language curriculum lens.\n\nThe unit should feel concrete, familiar, and lively. Food is one of the easiest bridges between school experience and children’s real daily life, so the non-language courses can build naturally around snack routines, pretend meals, serving, choice, caring, and playful food exploration.\n\nRather than trying to mirror the Power Up language unit too closely, the non-language unit should build a parallel early-years experience around:\n\n- familiar foods and drinks;\n- sharing and serving;\n- daily routines around eating and cleaning;\n- care for self and others during food-related moments;\n- curiosity, making, movement, and imaginative play linked to food.\n\nThe overall aim is not formal academic teaching. The aim is meaningful participation through hands-on, emotionally safe, age-appropriate food experiences across different course tracks.",
